								<div class="title" id="post-title-3573656">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005044&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606034552" alt="Decibelles" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005044)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;" >Decibelles</a></span> <span class="odate time_1503079515 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Aug 2017 18:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3573656">
									

<p>I think you could have something like that in a single essay. I think an ideal way to teach people how to write narratives is to look and analyze SCPs with stories conventional and unconventional (2188 and 106, for example), what makes them work, <em>why they work</em> and <em>what the reader can take away from it for their own writing</em>. Do this with enough SCPs, and you not only teach the person reading the essay what makes SCPs work really, really well (even the popular ones people don't think are that good like 106, 049, 012, 173 etc.), but how they apply that to their own writing, and consequently teach them critical thinking skills for whatever writing they read, SCP or not. This is partly why good advice is &quot;read, read, read, and read some more&quot;, because by reading a lot, you get a gradual innate sense of what works and why it works. But that's not the half of it; you then need to be able to articulate why it works, what makes it well-liked, and how you can apply that to yourself for writing. This also helps to develop critical thinking and allows people to more succinctly develop their own tastes and opinions on what's good and not, and overall improve the quality of the site.</p>
<p>This is all conjecture, of course, but I'm adamant it can be done. That's why I don't think this essay actually works; because it teaches you that narrative-based SCPs are a thing and that writing them is a good way to become a better writer, but not <em>how to do so</em> or <em>what makes them work</em>. It's very plausible to see a situation where a newbie reads this, and then writes a narrative-based SCP that's still low-quality because they didn't understand what makes them actually work.</p>

							</div>

								
					
