    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-3573847">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-3573847">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,3573847)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-3573847">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="3122708"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1503109998 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Aug 2017 02:33</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3573847">
									

<p>I don't want to give the implication that I didn't enjoy reading this essay, but I also feel like I disagree pretty strongly with part of its central premise: With maybe only one or two exceptions, every SCP article I've written started with a concept (&quot;A thing that does a thing&quot;) that I eventually &#8212; after quite a bit of struggling &#8212; built a narrative around.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Examples</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>SCP-3034 was just &quot;A girl is counting down to zero and we don't know why&quot;; from that, I eventually figured out some story about a Cold-War era Russian super-weapon. SCP-3035 was &quot;those bugs that really creeped me out from that movie, but emulating scientists&quot;. SCP-3043 is literally a character I used to play in Mage: The Ascension (and, briefly, Planescape). SCP-3045 was just &quot;I think it'd be hilarious if bees tried to run Google, and made weird compression algorithms&quot;. SCP-2639 was &quot;I really love the idea of kids trapped in a video game; is there a narrative I can wrap that around which would make the idea compelling?&quot;.</p>
<p>Notably, in a few of these cases, the story I ended up going with was <em>much different</em> than what I expected it to be. I thought SCP-3045 would end up just being pure comedy, but it ended up being kind of horror-esque; I expected SCP-2639 would be more about the Foundation exploiting a bunch of oblivious kids who never realized they were trapped in a game, but it ended up being about those kids figuring stuff out for themselves and taking (small, but notable) control of their lives.</p>
<p>When I write these things, I'm exploring a concept narratively &#8212; that's part of the fun for me.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>It's possible I'm just weird; part of the fun for me is taking an interesting concept and trying to create a story which can play with it. But I wanted to bring this up, because it's maybe the third or fourth time I've heard someone saying &quot;Story first, concept second&quot;, when for me it's mostly the other way around.</p>
<p>(Well, okay; to be fair, I often modify the concept a little to fit the story as I write it &#8212; so it's not that clear-cut. I guess what I'm getting at, though, is that &#8212; for me &#8212; it's definitely not &quot;Think of a story, then think of a concept that lets you tell that story&quot;. It's &quot;Think of a concept, then think of a story that you can tell with that concept&quot;)</p>

							</div>

								
