														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3576463">
									

<p>Although I agree that <em>narrative first</em> isn't necessary to get to a good article, understanding that <em>there needs to be a narrative</em> is vital. In all your examples, you started with the base idea, and then knew that it wasn't ready until you knew the story you wanted to tell with the pieces.</p>
<p>Many new authors fail because they fail to see the necessity of the narrative at all. Being able to explain that you can't expect the SCP to be complete by merely stating the existence of the set piece/character is a vital step to being able to successfully write for the site.</p>
<p>A &quot;narrative first&quot; approach can help new authors understand what will be expected in an SCP article. It's not the only way, but it's definitely a valid way.</p>
<p><strong>ETA:</strong> It's very similar to what I did with <a href="/scp-2459">SCP-2459</a>. I thought of a permanent traffic jam, thought it was interesting, but it wasn't until I linked it with our own social tendencies that I realized I had the story.</p>

							</div>

								
