					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2156983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606034552" alt="psul" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2156983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;" >psul</a></span> <span class="odate time_1503443893 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Aug 2017 23:18</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3576487">
									

<p>I agree with much of the criticism of this essay.</p>
<p>It would be one thing if it said &quot;try to include narrative in your article, as it makes it more unique and interesting to read&quot; - even then, it would be a far more useful essay if it analysed and gave examples of:</p>
<ul>
<li>some things other than narrative that can lead to successful articles (eg weirdness done right, atmosphere, puzzle-box structures, character); or</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>some things that people might not think of when they're told &quot;narrative&quot; (eg a series of test logs, a table of escalating effects, an extrapolation of the theme).</li>
</ul>
<p>But it's quite another thing when the article says that starting with an idea and trying to build a narrative around it is *wrong*. Especially when none of the examples given actually exemplify that statement - you haven't got the authors here stating that they started with the idea of a skip dealing with the trials of building a new society in the wild, and then they happened to think about that story being set in an Ikea.</p>
<p>And frankly I'd be surprised if that was the more common way of writing articles, even for Series III/IV. Again, taking WJS' example above, it wasn't &quot;let's write about social tendencies and groupthink&quot; and then &quot;traffic jam!&quot; - it was the other way around. Does anyone have an example of coming up with the narrative first, and then the central anomaly?</p>

							</div>

								
					
